% Explanations of failure (CLM-0031,
% docs/design/2026-11-11-explanations-of-failure.md): under --explain (the
% sibling failure.args passes it to the golden run) `false` is followed by
% the failure tree — every derivation the search tried, as a branch ending
% at the goal that failed and why: unsatisfiable, with the minimal set of
% earlier steps it conflicts with; no clause, with the candidates counted;
% a negation whose goal has a solution, with that solution's proof; a
% tabled call with no answer in its table. Facts of an integrity check:
eligible(X) :- { age(X) >= 18 }.
{ budget(acme) >= 1000 }.
k(c17, 5). k(c18, 2).
n(c17, 3). n(c18, 4).
violation(C) :- k(C, K), n(C, N), { K > N }.
claim(m1, residual_error, low).
p(1). p(2).
q(f(1)). q(f(2)). q(g(1)).
:- table anc/2.
parent(a, b).
anc(X, Y) :- parent(X, Y).
anc(X, Z) :- anc(X, Y), parent(Y, Z).
first(X) :- p(X) -> { X = 1 }.

?- eligible(bob), { age(bob) < 18 }.
?- violation(c18).
?- { budget(acme) < 500 }.
?- k(c99, K).
?- q(f(3)).
?- p(X), { X > 5 }.
?- \+ claim(m1, residual_error, _).
?- \+ p(3), { 1 > 2 }.
?- anc(b, Z).
?- first(3).
?- violation(c17).
